{"id":"71c95998-5a08-4787-ad2c-325066d88b7c","object":"chat.completion","created":1785019743,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not nodes or required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing nodes or required_symbols')\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    req_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in req_set:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_children = False\\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid in retained:\\n                has_retained_children = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in req_set and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement a function `solve(case)` that takes a payload (dict with nodes and required_symbols) and returns an object with `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids` as arrays of strings. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction: given a tree of nodes (each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), and required symbols, we need to decide which removable nodes to remove (in order) such that after removal, the tree still contains all required symbols somewhere (i.e., each required symbol appears in at least one node that is retained). The root is always retained (not removable). We need to simulate a reduction algorithm: we consider nodes in order: deeper nodes before shallower, and for ties in depth, use node ID as tie-break (stable). For each node (starting from deepest), if it is removable, we tentatively remove it, but only if all required symbols remain represented after removal. If the condition is satisfied, we actually remove it and record it in the removal order. Otherwise we retain it. At the end, we collect retained node IDs (those not removed) and the removed node IDs in the order they were removed.\n\nImportant: The node structure defines parent relationships. We need to know the tree structure to check if all required symbols remain after a removal. The removal of a node implies removing the node itself and its subtree? Or just the node? The specification says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Removing a node would remove it from the tree; but does it remove its children? In syntax-guided reduction (like Perses), removing a node typically means deleting that node and promoting its children? Or just removing the node? The problem statement: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests we cannot remove a node if it has retained children (i.e., children that are retained). So removal is only for leaf nodes or nodes whose children are also removed? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if a node has at least one child that is retained, we cannot remove that node. So when we remove a node, we also remove its children? Typically in tree reduction, removing a node might mean we cut the subtree. The condition \"Never remove a node with retained children\" implies that to remove a node, all its descendants must also be removed (or be considered removed). So the removal is effectively a subtree removal. But then we need to check that after removal, required symbols still exist elsewhere. So we need to decide removal order: deeper nodes first. That suggests we process nodes from deepest, and if a node is removable, we consider removing it. But if it has children, they might have been removed already (or not). The condition \"node with retained children\" means if any child is not removed yet, we cannot remove the parent. So the algorithm: process nodes in order of decreasing depth (and tie by ID), for each node: if it is removable and it has no retained children (i.e., all children have been removed or are not yet considered? Actually children are deeper, so they will be processed before the parent. When we process a child, we may decide to remove it or retain it. If we decide to retain it, then when we later consider the parent, the parent has a retained child, so we cannot remove the parent. If we decide to remove the child, then later the parent may be removable. So we need to keep track of which nodes are retained. Initially all nodes are retained. We'll iterate nodes in reverse depth order (deepest first). For each node, if it is removable and all its children are not retained (i.e., have been removed), then tentatively remove the node, but only if all required symbols are still present in the set of retained nodes (after removal). If condition holds, we actually remove it (mark as removed, add to removed list). Otherwise we keep it retained.\n\nBut we also have the condition \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So even if removable, if the node's symbol is required, we cannot remove it. Also root is always not removable (removable=false). So we should skip nodes that are not removable, or whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nAlso need to handle ties: at equal depth, use node ID as tie-breaker (stable). So we sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID (ascending? presumably stable order). Since IDs are strings, lexical order works.\n\nNow, how to check if all required symbols remain? We need to consider the set of nodes that are retained (including the current node if we don't remove it). After tentatively removing the current node, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there exists at least one retained node with that symbol. Required symbols are strings like \"root\", \"s1\", etc. Also note that root is required, but it's not removable anyway.\n\nWe also have utility values, but not used in the algorithm? The description says \"utility\" but the reduction policy seems to be based on removable flag and required symbols, not utility. So ignore utility.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). Input case is a dict with keys \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings). We need to produce output as per schema: { \"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...] }.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Validate input: ensure \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\" present, nodes list not empty, each node has required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (utility may not be needed). We can assume valid per contract.\n\n2. Build a dict node_by_id. Also build a map from parent_id to list of children IDs.\n\n3. Determine required symbols set.\n\n4. Initialize retained set = set of all node IDs.\n\n5. Initialize removed list = [].\n\n6. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID (lexicographic). Use node IDs for tie-breaking.\n\n7. For each node in sorted order:\n   - id = node[\"id\"]\n   - if id not in retained: skip (already removed)\n   - if not node[\"removable\"]: skip\n   - if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove required symbol)\n   - Check if any child of this node is still retained: for child_id in children[id]: if child_id in retained, then skip (has retained children)\n   - Tentatively remove: check if after removing this node, all required symbols still exist. Compute: temp_retained = retained - {id}. For each sym in required_symbols, if there is at least one node in temp_retained with that symbol? But we need to check all nodes. We can precompute symbol counts or have a dict of symbol->set of retained node ids. Since we remove one node at a time, we can maintain a count of required symbols. Simpler: for each required symbol, check if there is any retained node (other than this node) with that symbol. If node has that symbol, then after removal we must have at least one other retained node with that symbol. So count symbols: precompute a dict symbol_to_ids, then for each removal, decrement count for that node's symbol. If the count becomes zero, invalid. Also need to consider that the removed node might not be the only one for a required symbol. So we can maintain a Counter of symbol counts among retained nodes. Initially compute from all nodes. Then for each removal attempt, decrement count for the node's symbol, if that symbol is required and count becomes 0, then invalid, revert. If valid, we update retained set and removed list, and update symbol counts accordingly.\n\n   - But careful: we also need to consider that required symbols might be present in nodes that are already removed? Actually we only care about retained nodes. So we maintain counts only for retained nodes.\n\n   - After tentatively removing, we also need to consider that removing this node might affect the \"has retained children\" condition for its ancestors. But we only check ancestors later, so it's fine.\n\n   - If removal is allowed, we mark it as removed: remove from retained, add to removed list, decrement symbol count for node's symbol.\n\n8. After processing all nodes, we have removed list in removal order, and retained set. Then retained_node_ids should be sorted? The specification says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably lexicographically ascending). Removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (the actual removal order). So we need to output removed_node_ids as the list we built, and retained_node_ids as sorted(list(retained_set)).\n\nBut note: sorted retained IDs: we need to sort them. That is reasonable.\n\nAlso note: The algorithm may produce a situation where some required symbols only appear in nodes that are eventually removed? That shouldn't happen because we only remove if all required symbols still present. But the root is always retained (not removable). So at least root is there.\n\nEdge cases: What if a node is removable and has no children, but its symbol is not required, and removing it would still keep all required symbols? Then we remove it. That's fine.\n\nAlso, \"Never remove a node with retained children\" is enforced by the check before the tent removal.\n\nNow, we need to produce the implementation code. Also need to handle potential errors: if input is invalid (e.g., missing fields), raise ValueError.\n\nWe'll output as per the submission envelope: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a string.\n\nWe must ensure the code uses only standard library, no external imports (except maybe json for serialization, but we don't need to serialize output; we return a dict which will be serialized by evaluator). But we can import json if needed for validation? Not needed. Use only built-in types.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The node list might be unsorted; we need to build children map.\n\nAlso, we should handle the case where a node's parent is null? The root has parent null. Treat that as no parent. Also, we need to ensure we don't remove root, but it's already not removable.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\n    if not nodes or required_symbols is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing nodes or required_symbols\")\n\n    # Build node dict\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children map\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    # Initialize retained set\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    # Symbol counts among retained\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Required symbols set\n    req_set = set(required_symbols)\n    # Check root required? Not necessary but ensure root present.\n\n    # Sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n\n    removed = []\n\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in req_set:\n            continue\n        # Check children retained\n        has_retained_children = False\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\n            if cid in retained:\n                has_retained_children = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n\n        # Tentative removal: check required symbols\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        # Decrement symbol count if this node is the only one? Actually we need to check if after removal, all required symbols still have count >=1.\n        # We'll simulate by checking if sym is required and its count is 1, then removal would eliminate that required symbol.\n        # But also could be that sym is not required, then no issue.\n        # So:\n        removal_valid = True\n        if sym in req_set and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\n            # Would remove the last occurrence of a required symbol\n            removal_valid = False\n        # Note: there might be multiple required symbols; only the one in this node matters. So check only that.\n        if removal_valid:\n            # Actually we also need to consider if other required symbols are unaffected. But they are unaffected because we only change count for sym.\n            # So proceed.\n            # Remove node from retained\n            retained.remove(nid)\n            removed.append(nid)\n            # Update symbol count\n            symbol_count[sym] -= 1\n\n    # After loop, sort retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut we need to ensure that the order of removal matches the order we process nodes. Since we process deeper first, that's correct.\n\nPotential issue: What if a node has children that are also removable and we remove them first? Then when we get to the parent, its children are no longer in retained, so it passes the \"no retained children\" check. That's fine.\n\nAlso, note: The condition \"never remove a required symbol\" is checked by skipping if node symbol is in req_set.\n\nAlso, we need to handle the case where a node is not removable but its symbol is required? That's fine.\n\nNow, check with the given fixture. The fixture has nodes with removable flags. Let's simulate one case manually to see if logic matches expected? We don't have expected output, but we can reason.\n\nCase 0: nodes with required_symbols [\"root\",\"s1\"]. The root is always retained. \"s1\" appears in node n1 (depth1, not removable) and n4 (depth2, removable) and n6 (depth2, removable but symbol s0). So s1 is present in n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable). The algorithm will process nodes deeper first: n4 (depth2, removable, symbol s1) - but symbol s1 is required, so cannot remove. n5 (depth2, not removable), n6 (depth2, removable, symbol s0) - symbol s0 not required, no children? n6 has no children? Actually from list, children: n1 children? n1 has child n6? parent of n6 is n1, so n1 has child n6. n2 has child n4, n3 has child n5. So n6 is child of n1. n6 is removable, no children? It has no listed children. So n6 passes: removable, not required, no retained children (it has no children). Check required symbols: s0 not required, so removal valid. Remove n6. Then process depth1 nodes: n1 (depth1, not removable), n2 (depth1, removable, symbol s2), n3 (depth1, not removable). For n2: symbol s2 not required, but check children: n2 has child n4, which is still retained? n4 was not removed because required symbol. So n4 is retained, so n2 has retained children -> cannot remove n2. So n2 retained. n1 and n3 not removable. Then process depth0 root not removable. So removed list = [\"n6\"]; retained = all except n6: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]. That seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to ensure that when removing n6, we didn't affect any required symbol (s0 not required). Fine.\n\nCheck case 1 similar.\n\nNow, we must ensure the code is robust.\n\nPotential edge: if a node has multiple children and some are retained, we cannot remove it. That's correct.\n\nNow, regarding the \"grammar_valid\" condition: we are not actually checking grammar validity beyond the tree structure; the nodes are given, so we assume the tree is grammar-valid. The algorithm is just about reduction.\n\nNow, output: we return a dict. The evaluator will check that it matches the schema.\n\nNow, need to format the implementation as a string inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The string must be valid Python code, escaped properly. We'll write the code and then wrap it.\n\nWe'll also include error handling: raise ValueError for invalid input.\n\nWe'll not import anything.\n\nOne more thing: The spec says \"Do not mutate the input.\" We are not mutating case, we only read from it.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":4032,"total_tokens":6204,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3609},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}